---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="keyboard-shortcuts"
  description="Krova's keyboard shortcuts on Windows. In the 0.2 beta they use Ctrl+Alt: arrows move the focused window between zones, numbers send it to a zone, Shift swaps or applies a layout. Every shortcut can be changed or switched off. Plus the layout editor's keys."
  lead="On Windows, global shortcuts move the window you are working in from zone to zone. Every one can be changed or switched off. Inside the layout editor there are a few more keys. macOS has no global shortcuts yet."
>
  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <strong>This page describes the 0.2 beta.</strong> In v0.1.0 the shortcuts use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>
      with Left, Right, Up or 1 to 9, and the modifier keys are chosen in Settings, Keyboard shortcuts. The global shortcuts
      need Windows; on macOS you snap by dragging.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="global">The default shortcuts (Windows, 0.2 beta)</h2>
  <p>
    Every shortcut starts with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>. Adding <kbd>Shift</kbd> swaps windows or applies a layout.
    The window shortcuts act on the window that has the focus.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Global keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Keys</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+ arrow key</th>
          <td>Move the focused window to the neighbouring zone in that direction. Past the edge of the layout it goes to the nearest zone on the next monitor that way.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+ arrow key</th>
          <td>Swap it with the window in the neighbouring zone. A window Krova is not arranging just moves.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> to <kbd>9</kbd></th>
          <td>Move it to that zone number on its monitor.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> to <kbd>9</kbd></th>
          <td>Apply layout 1 to 9 (the order in the monitor&rsquo;s list) on the monitor under the cursor.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd></th>
          <td>Let a window in a zone fill the whole monitor; press again to put it back in its zone.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>]</kbd> / <kbd>[</kbd></th>
          <td>Next or previous window in the focused window&rsquo;s stack.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd></th>
          <td>Open the layout switcher on the monitor under the cursor.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd></th>
          <td>Pause or resume auto-arrange. Windows stay where they are.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd></th>
          <td>Turn focus dim on or off.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>/</kbd></th>
          <td>Show the cheat sheet: every shortcut, with your own keys. It is also under Shortcuts, Cheat sheet.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>How zones are numbered</h3>
  <p>
    Zones are counted in reading order: top to bottom, and left to right within a row (rows are compared to the nearest 1%).
    In a 2x2 grid, 1 is top left, 2 top right, 3 bottom left and 4 bottom right. In Halves and Thirds, 1 is the leftmost zone.
    The number is the zone&rsquo;s place in that order, not an id you can see. With more than nine zones, <kbd>1</kbd> to
    <kbd>9</kbd> reach the first nine and the arrow keys reach the others. A number past the last zone does nothing.
  </p>

  <h3>Which windows they move</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The window with the focus. A maximized window is restored first, then moved into the zone.</li>
    <li>
      They do nothing for the desktop, the taskbar, minimized windows, windows of programs running as administrator, or
      Krova&rsquo;s own windows.
    </li>
    <li>They follow the same settings as dragging: animated or instant moves, and the space between zones.</li>
    <li>When Krova is switched off, the shortcuts are released and do nothing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="change">Change a shortcut</h3>
  <p>
    Open Krova and choose <strong>Shortcuts</strong> in the sidebar. Click a shortcut and press the new keys;
    <kbd>Backspace</kbd> turns it off, and <strong>Reset all</strong> brings the defaults back. The four arrows of a row
    (move, swap) and the nine numbers (zones, layouts) change together. Before it saves a new combination, Krova checks it
    against its own shortcuts and asks Windows whether it is free. If it is taken, the row says so and offers up to three
    free combinations with the same key.
  </p>

  <h3>Known clashes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd> are back and forward in Visual Studio and JetBrains IDEs. While Krova runs, its shortcut wins.</li>
    <li>Some older Intel graphics drivers use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+ arrow keys to rotate the screen. Windows then refuses the shortcut and its row says so.</li>
    <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> is used by some launchers and quick-entry tools.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you chose <kbd>Win</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd> or <kbd>Win</kbd>+<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> in v0.1, the beta keeps those keys for the
    shortcuts that existed then. If a shortcut does nothing, see <a href={url('/support/#shortcut')}>Troubleshooting</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="editor">In the layout editor</h2>
  <p>
    These work while the editor window has focus. On a Mac, <kbd>Cmd</kbd> takes the place of <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>. The
    single-letter keys are ignored while a text box has focus, so you can still type a layout name.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Editor keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Esc</kbd></th><td>Back to the Select tool; if you are already there, clear the selected zone</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>V</kbd></th><td>Split vertically tool: the divider is a vertical line, so zones end up side by side</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>H</kbd></th><td>Split horizontally tool: zones end up stacked</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Delete</kbd></th><td>Delete the selected zone; a neighbour that shares a full edge takes its space</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd></th><td>Undo the last edit to the current layout</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd></th><td>Save the layout</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>On a focused divider</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Divider keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Arrow keys</th><td>Move the divider by 1%</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + arrow keys</th><td>Move the divider by 5%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>On a zone</h3>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Zone keyboard shortcuts">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd></th><td>Select the zone (Select tool)</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Arrow keys</th><td>Move the split guide (Split tool); hold <kbd>Shift</kbd> for 5% steps</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><kbd>Enter</kbd></th><td>Split the zone at the guide (Split tool)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Tabs and the sidebar</h3>
  <p>
    On the monitor tabs, <kbd>Left</kbd>, <kbd>Right</kbd>, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> move between monitors. In
    the beta&rsquo;s sidebar, <kbd>Up</kbd>, <kbd>Down</kbd>, <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> move between views. (In
    v0.1, <kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd> move between Editor and Settings.)
  </p>

  <h2 id="activation">The activation key is a separate setting</h2>
  <p>
    The key you hold while <em>dragging</em> (Shift by default) is not one of these shortcuts. It only shows the zones
    during a drag. Change it under Arrange, Snapping, Activation modifier (in v0.1: Settings, Snapping): Shift, Ctrl, Alt, or the Windows key (Command on a
    Mac). See <a href={url('/guides/first-snap/')}>Your first snap</a>.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
